findings.suggestion:
Restore proxy enforcement across redirects. Re-add a `beforeRedirect` handler on the request options that re-applies the proxy host/port/path and Proxy-Authorization to the redirect request, e.g.:

```js
if (proxy) {
  options.hostname = proxy.host;
  options.host = proxy.host;
  options.port = proxy.port;
  options.path = protocol + '//' + parsed.hostname + (parsed.port ? ':' + parsed.port : '') + options.path;

  if (proxy.auth) {
    var base64 = Buffer.from(proxy.auth.username + ':' + proxy.auth.password, 'utf8').toString('base64');
    options.headers['Proxy-Authorization'] = 'Basic ' + base64;
  }

  // Keep redirects inside the proxy tunnel
  options.beforeRedirect = function beforeRedirect(redirection) {
    redirection.headers.host = redirection.host;
    redirection.hostname = proxy.host;
    redirection.host = proxy.host;
    redirection.port = proxy.port;
    redirection.path = redirection.href;
    if (proxy.auth) {
      var b64 = Buffer.from(proxy.auth.username + ':' + proxy.auth.password, 'utf8').toString('base64');
      redirection.headers['Proxy-Authorization'] = 'Basic ' + b64;
    }
  };
}
```

Also restore the deleted regression test `SNYK-JS-AXIOS-1038255.js` (or an equivalent) so CI fails if this protection regresses again. If the intent is to drop proxy-on-redirect behavior, it must be a documented, opt-in breaking change — not a silent removal of an SSRF guard.
